  "id": "suitcase-carry",
  "name": "Suitcase Carry",
  "definition": "A duration-tracked unilateral dumbbell carry in which one side-held implement creates both a continuous tendency to open the hand and a frontal-plane spinal lateral-flexion tendency while the athlete walks forward on a level surface with the upper arm at the side, neutral humeral and forearm rotation, the elbow held extended, and no grip assistance. The frontal family plane is grounded in resisted spine.lateralFlexion; resisted hand.fingerExtension remains an additional cross-joint sagittal demand rather than the plane basis. The trunk opposes the side-bending load without the contract claiming dynamic side-bending repetitions or left-versus-right muscle credit that the taxonomy cannot represent. Ellestad and McGill directly support unilateral carriage and the asymmetric trunk-control envelope. Loaded-grip, upper-limb, lower-limb, and soleus assignments outside their measured panels are explicitly anatomy-and-mechanics-derived. Holds, farmer carriage, marching in place, backward or turning paths, straps, rack, overhead, front-loaded, yoke, and lunge variants remain outside this contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "arnold-2010-lower-limb",
    "christophy-2012-lumbar-spine",
    "gaffney-2014-trapezius-subdivisions",
    "holzbaur-2005-upper-extremity",
    "mirakhorlo-2018-hand-wrist-model",
    "ellestad-2024-loaded-carry-muscle-activation",
    "mcgill-2013-one-two-hand-carry"
  ],
